;;
;; REPL/RedTeam/[RT-E]_Sequencing.repl — FAMILY E: sequencing & state.
;;
;; "Can a LEGAL operation, in an illegal ORDER, leave value locked or misdirected?"
;;
;; Nothing here bypasses a guard. Every call is one the caller is entitled to make. The question is
;; whether the protocol's assumptions about ORDER survive a caller who does not follow it.
;;
;; Run: cd REPL && pact "RedTeam/[RT-E]_Sequencing.repl"
;;
(load "../deploy-stage00.repl")
(load "../deploy-stage01.repl")
;;RT-E-002 needs a live SWP pool to continue a defpact against.
(load "../Stage_01/[6.2]_DPTF.repl")
(load "../Stage_01/[6.3]_SWP.repl")
(load "../deploy-stage02.repl")
(load "../Stage_02/[6.3]_STOAICO.repl")

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "RT-E-001 - steal the STOAICO dust sweep with a late contribution")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== RT-E-001 · 01 · the last-unclaimed sweep, and who counts as last ====
(print "--- [RT-E-001 · 01 · the last-unclaimed sweep, and who counts as last] ---")
;;<<RT-E-001>> FAMILY: E | STATUS: REFUSED
;;HYPOTHESIS: STOAICO pays the LAST unclaimed staker the whole remaining vault rather than their
;;            computed share -- a dust sweep, so nothing is stranded. "Last" is decided by
;;            <unclaimed-count>, which is set to <nzs-count> ONLY at inject. If a contribution
;;            recorded mid-round moves nzs-count without moving unclaimed-count, a fresh account
;;            owed nothing should be able to collect while the counter still reads 1, and take the
;;            entire remaining supply out from under the staker it belongs to.
;;METHOD:     drive the suite to its end state (one staker collected, one outstanding, the whole
;;            690.53 owed to her), then have the admin record a legitimate late contribution of
;;            1.0 v-USD for a NEW participant, and try to collect as that participant.
;;RESULT:     the counters DO diverge and the reader DOES offer the whole vault to an account owed
;;            zero -- but the collect is refused one layer down. Attack fails. Detail below.
;;
;;THE HALF THAT IS TRUE, and it is worth pinning on its own. After the late stake:
;;
;;    unclaimed-count = 1      nzs-count = 3       <- they have diverged, exactly as predicted
;;    URC_AvailableRewards(newcomer)  = 0.000000000000     <- owed nothing
;;    URC_ClaimableRewards(newcomer)  = 690.525983513596   <- offered the ENTIRE vault
;;
;;So `URC_ClaimableRewards` will hand the whole remaining supply to an account with no claim on it.
;;That value is real and it is wrong; it is simply not reachable through the collect path.
;;
;;WHAT STOPS IT, and it is deliberate rather than lucky. `A_Stake`'s insert for a new contributor
;;stamps <last-collected-round> with the CURRENT distribution-round:
;;
;;    ;;#1C: new contributor starts at the CURRENT distribution-round, so a (mis-ordered)
;;    ;;     post-inject stake is not eligible for the already-injected round.
;;    (UDC_UserData 0.0 0 (UR_Global6) 0.0 (UR_Global11) account)
;;
;;and `STOAICO|REDEEM-CONTRIBUTION` enforces `(< last-collected-round distribution-round)`. A
;;newcomer is therefore born already-collected for the round they joined. Someone modelled this
;;exact attack and closed it at the eligibility layer.
;;
;;WHY THIS IS STILL WORTH A BLOCK RATHER THAN A SHRUG. The theft is prevented by ONE stamp, in a
;;different function from the one that computes the dangerous number, with no assertion previously
;;connecting them. `URC_ClaimableRewards` is also read by presentation paths, where it will state a
;;figure the account cannot have. If that stamp is ever changed -- a new contributor starting at
;;round 0, say, which reads like a harmless initialisation -- this becomes a live theft primitive
;;with no other guard in the way. The assertions below pin BOTH halves, so the day the stamp moves,
;;the first two go red and name what broke.
(env-sigs
    [
        { "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }
        { "key": "PK_LPAD", "caps": [] }
        { "key": "PK_Emma", "caps": [] }
        { "key": "PK_Lumy", "caps": [] }
        ;;THE ATTACKER'S OWN KEY. Without it the collect is refused by CAP_EnforceAccountOwnership
        ;;with `Keyset failure (keys-all)` -- a refusal that has NOTHING to do with the defence
        ;;under test. The first run of this block did exactly that, and a bare `expect-failure`
        ;;would have recorded a defence that does not exist. This is the family rule earning its
        ;;place: a red-team refusal must name its message.
        { "key": "PK_Byta", "caps": [] }
    ]
)
(let
    (
        (ref-CPAD:module{CitizenLaunchpadTalosV2} TS02-CPAD)
        (patron:string KST.ANHD)
        (victim:string KST.LUMY)
        (newcomer:string KST.BYTA)
    )
    (let
        (
            (supply-before:decimal (STOAICO.UR_Global4))
            (victim-owed:decimal (STOAICO.URC_AvailableRewards victim))
            (unclaimed-before:integer (STOAICO.UR_Global7))
            (nzs-before:integer (STOAICO.UR_Global5))
        )
        ;;the setup this attack needs: exactly one staker outstanding, and the whole vault is hers.
        (map print
            [
                (format "<<RT-E-001>> pre: unclaimed={} nzs={} supply={} owed-to-victim={}"
                    [unclaimed-before nzs-before supply-before victim-owed])
                (expect "<<RT-E-001>> pre: exactly one staker is unclaimed, so the sweep is armed"
                    1 unclaimed-before)
                ;;"the whole vault is hers" to within the per-collect floor: URC_AvailableRewards
                ;;floors at STOA_PREC, so it trails the raw supply by dust (~2.4e-8 here). Asserted
                ;;as a bound rather than an equality, because the dust is the POINT of the sweep.
                (expect "<<RT-E-001>> pre: and effectively the whole remaining vault is owed to HER"
                    true (and (<= victim-owed supply-before)
                              (> victim-owed (* 0.9999 supply-before))))
            ]
        )
        ;;THE ATTACK. A legitimate admin action -- recording a late contribution -- performed in the
        ;;wrong ORDER relative to the outstanding collect.
        (STOAICO.A_Stake patron newcomer 1.0)
        (map print
            [
                (format "<<RT-E-001>> post-stake: unclaimed={} nzs={} newcomer-owed={} newcomer-OFFERED={}"
                    [(STOAICO.UR_Global7) (STOAICO.UR_Global5)
                     (STOAICO.URC_AvailableRewards newcomer)
                     (STOAICO.URC_ClaimableRewards newcomer)])
                ;;1] the divergence is real: nzs moved, unclaimed did not.
                (expect "<<RT-E-001>> the two counters DO diverge -- nzs rose, unclaimed did not"
                    [(+ nzs-before 1) unclaimed-before]
                    [(STOAICO.UR_Global5) (STOAICO.UR_Global7)])
                ;;2] THE DEFECT, NOW FIXED -- and this assertion is the inversion of the one that
                ;;   found it. It used to read
                ;;
                ;;       [0.0 supply-before]      i.e. owed nothing, OFFERED the entire vault
                ;;
                ;;   because URC_ClaimableRewards branched on `(= (UR_Global7) 1)` alone --
                ;;   unclaimed-count, a property of the VAULT -- and used it as an answer about the
                ;;   ACCOUNT. Fixed 2026-09-14 (owner ruling) by URC_IzDustSweepClaimant, which
                ;;   also requires that THIS account is a real staker and has not already collected
                ;;   this round. The newcomer fails the second condition, so the two readers now
                ;;   agree, which is what they should always have done.
                (expect "<<RT-E-001>> the reader no longer offers the vault to an account owed zero"
                    [0.0 0.0]
                    [(STOAICO.URC_AvailableRewards newcomer)
                     (STOAICO.URC_ClaimableRewards newcomer)])
                ;;2b] NON-VACUITY, and it is essential here. Returning 0.0 to everyone would satisfy
                ;;    2] while destroying the dust sweep -- the whole point of the branch is that
                ;;    the LAST claimant is paid the remainder so rounding residue is never stranded.
                ;;    The victim IS that claimant, and still gets the whole vault.
                (expect "<<RT-E-001>> ...while the rightful last claimant is still paid the WHOLE vault"
                    [true supply-before]
                    [(STOAICO.URC_IzDustSweepClaimant victim)
                     (STOAICO.URC_ClaimableRewards victim)])
                (expect "<<RT-E-001>> ...and the newcomer is correctly NOT the sweep claimant"
                    false (STOAICO.URC_IzDustSweepClaimant newcomer))
                ;;3] ...and the single thing that stops it being theft: the newcomer is born
                ;;   already-collected for the round they joined. Message checked, per the family
                ;;   rule -- "refused" proves nothing if the refusal came from somewhere else.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-E-001>> ATTACK REFUSED: a newcomer is born already-collected for this round"
                    "has already collected this distribution round"
                    (ref-CPAD::STOAICO|C_Collect patron newcomer))
                ;;4] and the refusal is TOTAL -- the vault is untouched, so the victim's claim
                ;;   survives intact. Without this, 3] would also pass against a refusal that
                ;;   happened after a partial payout.
                (expect "<<RT-E-001>> ...and the vault is untouched, so the rightful claim survives"
                    supply-before (STOAICO.UR_Global4))
            ]
        )
    )
    ""
)
(env-sigs [])
(rollback-tx)
;;||>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
;;|| NEXT                   >
;;||>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
(begin-tx "RT-E-002 - continue a defpact you did not start")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 100000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== RT-E-002 · 01 · whose signature does step 1 require? ====
(print "--- [RT-E-002 · 01 · whose signature does step 1 require?] ---")
;;<<RT-E-002>> FAMILY: E | STATUS: REFUSED
;;HYPOTHESIS: a defpact's steps are separate transactions, so the signer set can CHANGE between
;;            them. `20_MTX-SWP.pact` contains ZERO occurrences of CAP_EnforceAccountOwnership or
;;            CAP_Owner -- the MTX pacts never check who is driving them, and delegate ownership to
;;            the core ops the steps call. If step 1 does not re-establish the starter's authority,
;;            a STRANGER can continue someone else's add-liquidity pact and so CHOOSE THE MOMENT it
;;            executes. That matters because RT-F-001 established what step 1 does when the pool has
;;            moved: it takes the rollback branch and the starter loses the initiation fee. An
;;            attacker who can time the continuation can swap against the pool first and then
;;            continue the victim's pact into its own failure branch -- turning RT-F-001's accident
;;            into a directed grief.
;;METHOD:     start the pact as ANHD, then REPLACE the signer set with EMMA's alone and continue.
;;            One transaction, so the continuation resolves against the pact started here and no
;;            pact-id has to be guessed. The question is not only success or failure but WHICH
;;            guard answers -- a refusal from the wrong guard is still a latent defect.
;;RESULT:     REFUSED, on BOTH pact families -- and the manner is the finding. The stranger's
;;            continuation dies on a plain `Keyset failure (keys-all): [PK_Ancie...]`, naming the
;;            STARTER's key. Nothing in the defpact layer asked who was driving: the refusal comes
;;            from a DOWNSTREAM core op that happens to touch the starter's account, so the
;;            protection is a property of what the step DOES, not of the pact.
;;
;;            That is safe today and structurally thin. Every MTX step currently moves the
;;            starter's tokens, so every one demands their key. A future step that touched only
;;            protocol state -- a sweep, a recompute, a flag flip -- would have nothing to demand
;;            it, and this attack would succeed against that step without anything else changing.
;;            The attack is worth keeping precisely because it does not test a guard; it tests an
;;            ABSENCE, and the absence is still there.
;;
;;            NON-VACUITY is asserted in RT-E-002b: the same pact, continued by the account that
;;            started it, proceeds. A step that refused everyone would satisfy the refusal above
;;            and prove nothing -- RT-C-001's lesson.
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(let
    (
        (ref-TS01-CP:module{TalosStageOne_ClientPactsV4} TS01-CP)
        (ref-DALOS:module{OuronetDalosV2} DALOS)
        (patron:string KST.ANHD)
        (pool:string "S|DLK-98c486052a51|OURO-98c486052a51|DWK-98c486052a51")
        (amounts:[decimal] [10.0 10.0 10.0])
    )
    (let
        (
            (b0:decimal (ref-DALOS::UR_TF_AccountSupply patron false))
        )
        ;;step 0, legitimately, by the owner
        (ref-TS01-CP::SWP|C_AddStandardLiquidity patron patron pool amounts)
        ;;...and now a DIFFERENT signer entirely. EMMA has never touched this pact.
        (env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_Emma", "caps": [] }])
        (map print
            [
                (format "<<RT-E-002>> step 0 charged {} to the starter; continuing as EMMA"
                    [(- b0 (ref-DALOS::UR_TF_AccountSupply patron false))])
                ;;THE ATTACK. If this succeeds, continuation timing is attacker-controlled.
                ;;RESULT: REFUSED -- and by a plain keyset failure naming the STARTER's key.
                ;;Pinned by message, because the identity of the refusing guard is the finding.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-E-002>> a stranger CANNOT drive step 1 -- the starter's key is demanded"
                    "Keyset failure (keys-all): [PK_Ancie"
                    (continue-pact 1))
            ])
    )
    ""
)
(print "===^^^===")
(env-sigs [])
(rollback-tx)
;;||>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
;;|| NEXT                   >
;;||>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
(begin-tx "RT-E-002b - non-vacuity, and whether the refusal is structural or incidental")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 100000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [RT-E-002b · the owner can, and a second pact family] ---")
;;NON-VACUITY FIRST. A continuation that refuses EVERYONE is not a boundary, it is a broken step --
;;RT-C-001's lesson. The SAME pact, continued by the SAME account that started it, must proceed.
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(let
    (
        (ref-TS01-CP:module{TalosStageOne_ClientPactsV4} TS01-CP)
        (patron:string KST.ANHD)
        (pool:string "S|DLK-98c486052a51|OURO-98c486052a51|DWK-98c486052a51")
        (amounts:[decimal] [10.0 10.0 10.0])
    )
    (ref-TS01-CP::SWP|C_AddStandardLiquidity patron patron pool amounts)
    (map print
        [
            (expect "<<RT-E-002>> NON-VACUITY: the STARTER continues the same pact without trouble"
                true (!= "" (format "{}" [(continue-pact 1)])))
        ])
    ""
)
(print "===^^^===")
(env-sigs [])
(rollback-tx)
;;||>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
;;|| NEXT                   >
;;||>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
(begin-tx "RT-E-002c - a SECOND pact family, same question")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 100000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [RT-E-002c · is the refusal structural, or incidental to what the step touches?] ---")
;;THE STRUCTURAL QUESTION. RT-E-002 was refused by a plain `Keyset failure (keys-all)` naming the
;;STARTER's key -- not by anything in the defpact layer. `20_MTX-SWP.pact` contains ZERO
;;occurrences of CAP_EnforceAccountOwnership or CAP_Owner: no step asks who is driving it. The
;;refusal came from a DOWNSTREAM core op that happens to touch the starter's account.
;;
;;That distinction matters. If the protection is a property of what a step HAPPENS to do rather
;;than of the pact itself, then a future step that touches only protocol state -- a sweep, a
;;recompute, a state flip -- would have nothing to demand the starter's key, and a stranger could
;;drive it. So: ask the same question of a DIFFERENT pact family, whose step 1 does different work.
;;If both refuse, the practical answer today is "safe"; the structural answer is still "incidentally".
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(let
    (
        (ref-DALOS:module{OuronetDalosV2} DALOS)
        (patron:string KST.ANHD)
        (pool-tokens:[object{SwapperV4.PoolTokens}]
            ;;SSTOA leads: a STANDARD pool takes the amp = -1.0 branch, where SWPI::UEV_Issue
            ;;requires the FIRST token to BE a Principal (not merely neighbour one), and the pool
            ;;must clear a minimum worth in WSTOA. Both rules were measured in DEFPACT-BILLING.
            [ { "token-id": (ref-DALOS::UR_SilverStoaID), "token-supply": 2000.0}
              { "token-id": "AUSD-98c486052a51", "token-supply": 50000.0}
              { "token-id": "CUSD-98c486052a51", "token-supply": 50000.0} ])
    )
    (let
        (
            (split-stoa:[decimal]
                (ref-DALOS::URC_SplitSTOAPrices patron
                    (+ (ref-DALOS::UR_UsagePrice "dptf") (ref-DALOS::UR_UsagePrice "swp"))))
        )
        (env-sigs
            [
                { "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }
                { "key": "6fa1d9c3e5078a54038159c9a6bd7182301e16d6f280615eddb18b8bd2d6c263"
                , "caps":
                    [ (coin.TRANSFER "k:6fa1d9c3e5078a54038159c9a6bd7182301e16d6f280615eddb18b8bd2d6c263"
                          "k:50d6c59b21e5e6e55baecaa75a1007de37576bde12d8230dc82459cc01b9484b" (at 2 split-stoa))
                      (coin.TRANSFER "k:6fa1d9c3e5078a54038159c9a6bd7182301e16d6f280615eddb18b8bd2d6c263"
                          "k:0cb30c0121ff919266121a99ff9359871818932211df94dae4137c29bc0e8f7e" (at 0 split-stoa))
                      (coin.TRANSFER "k:6fa1d9c3e5078a54038159c9a6bd7182301e16d6f280615eddb18b8bd2d6c263"
                          "c:XM-pkmuB5XUQlp87ZYSbfKt8qzmHY6O2EHAzMRVBt3k" (at 3 split-stoa))
                      (coin.TRANSFER "k:6fa1d9c3e5078a54038159c9a6bd7182301e16d6f280615eddb18b8bd2d6c263"
                          "c:iQQFWj6gWtpGEzhM_O5ekW1QtnQQy55R8BRPGhj_0FU" (at 1 split-stoa)) ] }
            ])
        (TS01-CP.SWP|C_IssueStandardPool patron patron pool-tokens 10.0 false)
        ;;...and now a stranger, exactly as in RT-E-002.
        (env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_Emma", "caps": [] }])
        (map print
            [
                (expect-failure "<<RT-E-002>> a stranger cannot continue the ISSUE pact either"
                    "Keyset failure (keys-all): [PK_Ancie"
                    (continue-pact 1))
            ])
    )
    ""
)
(print "===^^^===")
(env-sigs [])
(rollback-tx)
